@* The picture next to a product on the public shop — or, when there is no picture,
   a placeholder that looks deliberate instead of a broken-image glyph.

   Most of the catalogue has no photo yet (that is the standing launch blocker), so
   this is not an edge case: it is what most cards, the cart, and most detail pages
   actually render. The placeholder is a monogram of the product's first letter on a
   tint whose hue is derived from the name, so a wall of photoless products reads as a
   designed grid rather than a row of identical grey boxes.

   The letter is drawn as SVG <text>, not a styled character, so ONE component scales
   from an 82px cart thumbnail to a half-page detail image without a font-size per call
   site — the glyph is a fixed fraction of the box at every size. No JS: the shop is
   static SSR and must render correctly before (or without) a circuit. *@

@if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Src))
{
    <img class="@Class" src="@Src" alt="@Alt" loading="@Loading" />
}
else
{
    <span class="@($"shop-ph {Class}")" style="--ph-h:@_hue" role="img" aria-label="@Alt">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true">
            <text x="20" y="21" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central">@_initial</text>
        </svg>
    </span>
}

@code {
    [Parameter] public string? Src { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string Alt { get; set; } = "";
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }
    /// Above-the-fold images (the detail page's main photo) pass "eager"; grids stay lazy.
    [Parameter] public string Loading { get; set; } = "lazy";

    private string _initial = "•";
    private int _hue = 330;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        var name = (Alt ?? string.Empty).Trim();
        _initial = name.Length > 0 ? char.ToUpperInvariant(name[0]).ToString() : "•";

        // A stable hue per name: same product, same colour every render, no lookup table.
        var h = 0;
        foreach (var ch in name) h = (h * 31 + ch) % 360;
        _hue = h;
    }
}
